## Proof

**Claim:** The universal cover of $Gr_2(4)$ is $S^2 \times S^2$.

### Step 1. Fundamental group of $Gr_2(4)$

The Grassmannian of (unoriented) 2-planes in $\mathbb{R}^4$ has the homogeneous-space presentation
$$Gr_2(4) \cong O(4)\big/\big(O(2)\times O(2)\big).$$
For $0 < k < n$, the standard fibration
$$O(k)\times O(n-k) \longrightarrow O(n) \longrightarrow Gr_k(n)$$
and the long exact sequence in homotopy (using $\pi_1(O(n))\cong \mathbb{Z}/2$ for $n\geq 3$ and the map $\pi_1(O(2)\times O(2))\to \pi_1(O(4))$ being surjective with kernel of index $2$) give
$$\pi_1\big(Gr_k(n)\big)\cong \mathbb{Z}/2.$$
In particular $\pi_1(Gr_2(4))\cong \mathbb{Z}/2$, so the universal cover is a **double** cover.

### Step 2. The oriented Grassmannian is the candidate double cover

Define the *oriented* Grassmannian
$$\widetilde{Gr}_2(4) \;=\; SO(4)\big/\big(SO(2)\times SO(2)\big),$$
the space of 2-planes together with a choice of orientation. Forgetting the orientation gives a 2-sheeted covering map
$$p:\widetilde{Gr}_2(4)\longrightarrow Gr_2(4).$$
If $\widetilde{Gr}_2(4)$ is simply connected, then it is the universal cover.

### Step 3. Diffeomorphism $\widetilde{Gr}_2(4)\cong S^2\times S^2$

Equip $\mathbb{R}^4$ with its Euclidean metric and orientation, and consider the Hodge star
$$\ast:\Lambda^2\mathbb{R}^4\longrightarrow \Lambda^2\mathbb{R}^4,\qquad \ast^2 = +1.$$
Thus $\Lambda^2\mathbb{R}^4 = \Lambda^2_+\oplus \Lambda^2_-$ splits into self-dual ($\ast=+1$) and anti-self-dual ($\ast=-1$) summands, each of dimension $3$.

An **oriented** 2-plane $P$ determines a unit *simple* 2-form $\omega_P\in \Lambda^2\mathbb{R}^4$ (the volume form of $P$ with its chosen orientation), characterized by
$$\|\omega_P\|=1,\qquad \omega_P\wedge \omega_P = 0.$$
Writing $\omega_P = \omega_P^+ + \omega_P^-$ with $\omega_P^\pm\in \Lambda^2_\pm$, the simplicity condition forces
$$\|\omega_P^+\| = \|\omega_P^-\| = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}},$$
so each of $\omega_P^+$ and $\omega_P^-$ is a vector of fixed length in a 3-dimensional space, i.e. a point of a 2-sphere (after rescaling by $\sqrt{2}$). Conversely, any pair $(u,v)\in \Lambda^2_+\times \Lambda^2_-$ with $\|u\|=\|v\|=1/\sqrt{2}$ combines to a unit simple 2-form, hence an oriented 2-plane. This gives a diffeomorphism
$$\widetilde{Gr}_2(4)\;\xrightarrow{\;\sim\;}\; S^2_+\times S^2_-,\qquad P\longmapsto \big(\sqrt{2}\,\omega_P^+,\;\sqrt{2}\,\omega_P^-\big).$$

### Step 4. Simple connectivity

Since $S^2$ is simply connected ($\pi_1(S^2)=0$),
$$\pi_1\big(\widetilde{Gr}_2(4)\big)\cong \pi_1(S^2\times S^2)\cong \pi_1(S^2)\times \pi_1(S^2)=0.$$
Thus $\widetilde{Gr}_2(4)\cong S^2\times S^2$ is simply connected and is a double cover of $Gr_2(4)$. Being simply connected, it is **the** universal cover.

### Conclusion

The universal cover of the Grassmann manifold $Gr_2(4)$ is
$$\boxed{S^2\times S^2}.$$

